// 六级 8000 词 · 每组配套短文 write.cet4（由 build/passages/write.cet4.txt 生成）
CET6.passages("write.cet4",{"1":{"w":["abandon","ability","absence","absorb","abundant","abuse","academic","accelerate","accept","acceptable","access","accident","accommodation","accompany","accomplish","accord","accordingly","account","accumulate","accurate"],"title":"Learning to Study at University","t":"For many students, the first year at university is a shock. In high school, teachers check homework every day; at university, students must take responsibility for their own learning. According to a recent survey, nearly a third of first-year students consider whether to **abandon** their course in the first semester.\n\nSuccess depends on more than intelligence. Students need the **ability** to manage time, set goals and **absorb** large amounts of information. **Academic** life offers **abundant** resources, such as libraries, online databases and study groups, but students must learn how to **access** them. Those who **accumulate** good habits early often **accelerate** their progress in later years.\n\nSome problems are common. Frequent **absence** from lectures is one of the biggest causes of failure. Another is the **abuse** of mobile phones in class, which makes it hard to concentrate. Universities **accordingly** encourage students to switch off their phones and take notes by hand. Experts say that **accurate** notes are one of the best tools for remembering what you learn.\n\nLiving conditions also matter. Finding comfortable **accommodation** can be stressful, especially for students from other cities. An **accident** or illness far from home can be frightening, so many universities ask older students to **accompany** newcomers during their first weeks. These mentors help explain the rules and give an honest **account** of student life.\n\nStudents should **accept** that mistakes are part of learning. A grade that seems barely **acceptable** at first can improve with effort. Most teachers are in **accord** on one point: what matters is not how fast you start, but what you **accomplish** by the end."},"2":{"w":["accustomed","achieve","achievement","acknowledge","acquire","action","active","activity","actually","adapt","add","addition","additional","address","adequate","adhere","adjust","admire","admit","adopt"],"title":"A Summer Volunteering in Africa","t":"Last summer, I spent two months volunteering at a primary school in rural Kenya. Before I left, my friends asked why I would give up my holiday. **Actually**, I wanted to do something meaningful, and I hoped the experience would help me **acquire** new skills.\n\nAt first, life was hard. I was not **accustomed** to the heat, the simple food or the long walks to school. I had to **adapt** quickly and **adjust** my expectations. There was no running water, and electricity was not always **adequate** for our needs. I had to **admit** that I felt homesick during the first week.\n\nThe school was full of energy. The children were **active** and curious, and every **activity**, from singing to drawing, made them laugh. I taught English and maths, and in **addition**, I helped organise a small library. Each week, I would **add** new books that we received from donors abroad. The teachers asked me to give **additional** lessons in the evenings for older students.\n\nI came to **admire** the local teachers greatly. They worked long hours for little pay, yet they always **adhered|adhere** to high standards. One teacher, Mrs. Otieno, had **adopted|adopt** two orphaned children and treated every pupil as her own. At the end of my stay, the school asked me to **address** the students at a small ceremony. I struggled to find words.\n\nLooking back, my biggest **achievement** was not teaching, but learning. I learned to **acknowledge** my limits and to take **action** even when things were difficult. If we want to **achieve** real change, I believe, we must first understand the lives of others."},"3":{"w":["adult","advance","advanced","advantage","advertise","advertisement","advice","advise","advocate","affair","affect","affection","afford","against","agency","agent","aggressive","agriculture","aid","aim"],"title":"Should Advertising to Children Be Limited?","t":"Every day, children see hundreds of advertisements on television, phones and the internet. Companies **advertise** toys, snacks and games, often using bright colours and cartoon characters. Many parents worry that this has a negative effect, and some experts **advocate** strict limits on advertising aimed at children.\n\nThere are clear reasons for concern. Children are not yet able to understand the purpose of an **advertisement** in the way an **adult** can. Studies suggest that advertising can **affect** their food choices, encouraging them to eat unhealthy snacks. Some advertisements also use **aggressive** techniques, such as telling children that they need a product to be popular.\n\nAdvertising also creates pressure on families. Children may ask for expensive products that parents cannot **afford**. Some companies even use the **affection** between parents and children to sell products, suggesting that a good parent buys the latest toy. Critics argue this is an unfair **advantage**.\n\nOn the other hand, some people argue **against** strict rules. They say that advertising pays for free children's programmes and that parents should simply **advise** their children. Advertising **agency** leaders insist that their **aim** is to inform, not to manipulate. Some advertisements even promote healthy habits, such as eating vegetables or supporting **agriculture**.\n\nIn my view, both sides have a point, but children need extra protection. Governments should treat the issue as a public **affair**, not just a private family matter. With every **advance** in technology and increasingly **advanced** data tools, advertisers can target children more precisely than ever. Parents, teachers and even the **agents|agent** who sell advertising space should work together, and educational programmes can **aid** children in understanding what they see. That, I believe, is the best **advice**."},"4":{"w":["airline","alarm","alcohol","alike","alive","allow","alone","alter","alternative","although","altitude","ambition","amount","analysis","analyze","ancestor","ancient","anger","announce","annual"],"title":"A Journey to the Roof of the World","t":"Last summer, my family travelled to Tibet, one of the highest regions on Earth. We flew with a domestic **airline** from Chengdu, and when we landed in Lhasa, the **altitude** was more than three thousand six hundred metres. Within hours, I felt dizzy and tired.\n\nOur guide warned us about altitude sickness. He advised us to drink a large **amount** of water and to avoid **alcohol** for the first few days. He would not **allow** anyone to climb stairs too quickly. \"Many tourists **alter** their plans because they move too fast,\" he said. One traveller in our hotel ignored the advice and had to return home early.\n\nTibet was full of **ancient** wonders. We visited the Potala Palace, built more than a thousand years ago, and monasteries where families light butter lamps in memory of each **ancestor**. Our guide gave a careful **analysis** of local history, and he taught us to **analyze** the symbols painted on the walls. Some festivals take place every year, and we were lucky to see an **annual** horse race.\n\nThe landscape made us feel small and **alone**. Mountains rose in every direction, and lakes shone like mirrors. **Although** life at high altitude is hard, the people we met were warm and full of energy. They made us feel **alive** in a way the city never does. Old and young **alike** welcomed us into their homes.\n\nOn our last day, we woke early when our **alarm** rang at four o'clock to watch the sunrise over the mountains. When the guide happened to **announce** that the bus had broken down, there was a moment of **anger**, but we soon found an **alternative**: we walked. The trip changed my **ambition**: I now dream of becoming a travel writer."},"5":{"w":["anticipate","anxiety","anxious","apartment","apologize","apology","apparent","appeal","appear","appearance","appetite","appliance","applicant","application","apply","appointment","appreciate","approach","appropriate","approval"],"title":"My First Job Application","t":"Last spring, I submitted my first serious job **application**. I wanted to **apply** for an internship at a large technology company, and the competition was fierce: more than a thousand people were interested. As the **applicant** with the least experience, I felt great **anxiety**.\n\nPreparing took weeks. I wrote my CV carefully and asked my teacher to check that it was **appropriate**. I tried to **anticipate** difficult questions and practised my answers aloud in my small **apartment**. My roommate laughed at me, but she also helped me plan my **appearance** for the interview: a simple suit and neat hair.\n\nOn the day of my **appointment**, I was so **anxious** that I lost my **appetite** and could not eat breakfast. To make things worse, I arrived late because my train was delayed. I had to **apologize** to the receptionist, who accepted my **apology** with a smile. It was **apparent** that she had seen many nervous students before.\n\nThe interview went better than I expected. The manager liked my **approach** to problem-solving, and I told her about a project in which I designed a smart home **appliance** that saves energy. She seemed to **appreciate** my honesty when I admitted what I did not know. She said the company was looking for people with curiosity, and that my answers had real **appeal**.\n\nA week later, I received an email. My internship had gained final **approval**! I could hardly believe it. That experience taught me an important lesson: confidence does not mean having no fear. It means choosing to **appear** in that room anyway and doing your best."},"6":{"w":["approve","approximately","architect","architecture","area","argue","argument","arise","arouse","arrange","arrangement","arrival","art","article","artificial","artist","artistic","ashamed","aside","aspect"],"title":"A New Museum for the City","t":"Last year, our city announced plans to build a new art museum near the river. The design, by a famous **architect**, was bold: a building of glass and steel shaped like a giant wave. The plan quickly became a hot topic, and people began to **argue** about it in newspapers and online.\n\nSupporters praised its **artistic** vision. They said modern **architecture** could make the city more attractive to tourists and give local **art** a new home. One local **artist** wrote an **article** describing the museum as \"a gift to future generations\". The project would cover **approximately** twenty thousand square metres, turning an empty industrial **area** into a cultural centre.\n\nCritics had a different **argument**. Some felt the design was too **artificial** and did not suit the old town. Others were worried about cost; they said they would be **ashamed** if the city spent so much money while schools needed repairs. Questions began to **arise** about who would pay for maintenance, and the debate seemed to **arouse** strong feelings on both sides.\n\nThe city council tried to listen to every **aspect** of the debate. It decided to **arrange** a series of public meetings, and each **arrangement** allowed residents to speak freely. After months of discussion, the council voted to **approve** a modified design that used local stone as well as glass.\n\nThe museum opened last month. On the day of its **arrival** in the city's cultural life, thousands of visitors queued outside. Many who had opposed the plan set their doubts **aside** and admitted that it was beautiful. Good public buildings, it seems, can bring a city together."},"7":{"w":["assemble","assess","assign","assignment","assist","assistant","associate","association","assume","assumption","assure","astonish","astronaut","athlete","atmosphere","attach","attack","attain","attempt","attend"],"title":"When an Astronaut Visited Our School","t":"Last spring, our school had a very special guest: a real **astronaut** who had spent six months on a space station. The whole school had to **assemble** in the sports hall, and the **atmosphere** was electric. Even the teachers seemed excited.\n\nThe astronaut began by describing life in space. Without gravity, she explained, you cannot simply **assume** that objects will stay where you put them; everything must be **attached|attach** to the walls. She showed a video of an **attempt** to drink water, which floated around her head like a silver ball. The audience laughed, and she said the sight would **astonish** anyone the first time.\n\nShe also spoke about her training. Like an Olympic **athlete**, she had to exercise every day and **attain** a very high level of fitness. Doctors would regularly **assess** her health. She said that every **assignment** in space was carefully planned, and that each team member would **assign** tasks to others according to their skills. \"In space, you must **assist** each other,\" she said. \"Your life depends on it.\"\n\nSome students asked difficult questions. One boy wanted to know whether astronauts are ever afraid of an **attack** of panic. She said yes, but that training helps you stay calm. Another student questioned the common **assumption** that only scientists can become astronauts. \"Not at all,\" she replied, and she was quick to **assure** us that people from many backgrounds work in space programmes.\n\nAfter the talk, her **assistant** handed out leaflets about a young scientists' **association** that students could join. Many of us **associate** space with films, but that day it felt real. Everyone wanted to **attend** next year's science camp."},"8":{"w":["attention","attentive","attitude","attract","attraction","attractive","attribute","audience","author","authority","automatic","automation","available","average","aviation","avoid","award","aware","awkward","bachelor"],"title":"Will Robots Take Our Jobs?","t":"Last month, a famous **author** came to our university to talk about her new book on **automation** and the future of work. More than five hundred students filled the hall, and she held our **attention** for two hours. Her book had recently won a national **award**, and it was easy to see why.\n\nShe began with a simple question: will robots take our jobs? Many people **attribute** rising unemployment to technology, she said, but the reality is more complex. **Automatic** machines have already replaced many factory workers, and in **aviation**, computers now fly planes for most of each journey. Yet new jobs are also being created that did not exist twenty years ago.\n\nThe author argued that our **attitude** matters most. Workers who are **aware** of changes and willing to learn will **attract** new opportunities. Those who **avoid** new skills may find themselves left behind. She pointed out that the **average** worker will change careers several times in a lifetime, and that a **bachelor** degree is no longer enough on its own.\n\nShe also discussed the role of government. Public **authority**, she said, must make sure that training is **available** to everyone, not only the rich. Otherwise, the benefits of technology will be shared unequally. This idea had obvious **attraction** for the young **audience**, many of whom worry about their future.\n\nAt the end, there was an **awkward** moment when a student asked whether her own job could be done by a computer. She laughed and said, \"Perhaps, but it would not be as **attentive** to its readers.\" Her honest, friendly answer made the talk even more **attractive**."},"9":{"w":["background","backward","bacteria","badly","baggage","bake","balance","ban","barely","bargain","barrier","base","basic","basically","basin","basis","bathroom","battery","battle","beach"],"title":"A Beach Holiday Without Plastic","t":"Last summer, my family spent a week at a small seaside town in southern China. The **beach** was beautiful, but I was shocked to see plastic bottles and bags along the shore. A local volunteer explained that plastic waste was **badly** damaging the sea, killing fish and spreading harmful **bacteria**.\n\nThe town had recently introduced a **ban** on single-use plastic. Shops no longer gave out plastic bags, and restaurants used paper straws. At first, some visitors complained. They were **barely** used to life without plastic, and a few called the rules **backward**. But most people soon adjusted.\n\nThe rules were simple and practical. **Basically**, tourists were asked to bring their own bags and bottles. Every hotel **bathroom** offered refillable soap instead of tiny plastic bottles. Even the airport asked travellers to reduce plastic in their **baggage**. A **basic** guide, available in four languages, explained the reasons for the ban, and even the lamps along the seafront ran on a solar **battery**.\n\nNot everyone found it easy. Small shop owners worried about costs, and one woman who liked to **bake** cakes for tourists had to find new packaging. For her, the biggest **barrier** was price. However, the town offered a small subsidy, and many local businesses now see eco-friendly products as a good **bargain** in the long run.\n\nThe town's success is built on a strong **base** of community support. Most residents come from fishing families, and their **background** gives them a deep love of the sea. They see the fight against plastic as a **battle** to protect their future. They also hope to find the right **balance** between tourism and nature, and to keep the whole river **basin** behind the town clean. Their efforts, one volunteer told me, are the **basis** of a healthier coast."},"10":{"w":["beauty","behalf","behave","behavior","belief","belong","belt","beneficial","benefit","besides","beyond","bicycle","bill","billion","biology","birth","bitter","blame","blank","blend"],"title":"The Bike-Sharing Debate","t":"Ten years ago, few people in Chinese cities rode a **bicycle** to work. Today, millions use shared bikes every day. The **birth** of bike-sharing has changed city life, but it has also caused heated debate.\n\nThe **benefits|benefit** are obvious. Shared bikes are cheap, convenient and **beneficial** to the environment. A short ride costs less than a bus ticket, and users never have to worry about a repair **bill**. Companies have invested more than a **billion** yuan in the industry. **Besides** saving money, cycling improves health and reduces traffic.\n\nHowever, there are problems. Some users **behave** badly, leaving bikes on pavements or damaging them. This kind of **behavior** has made some residents **bitter**, and they **blame** the companies for not managing their bikes properly. Others argue that the responsibility does not **belong** to companies alone; citizens must also follow the rules.\n\nExperts suggest a **blend** of solutions. Cities can create special parking areas, and companies can use technology to track bikes. A professor of **biology** at a local university pointed out that cycling also benefits air quality and public health, an argument that goes **beyond** convenience. On **behalf** of many residents, local leaders have asked for clearer rules, and some companies now give riders a reflective **belt** to wear when cycling at night.\n\nIn my opinion, bike-sharing reflects a simple **belief**: cities should be designed for people, not cars. When I first rode a shared bike, my mind was **blank**; I did not know where to park or how to unlock it. Now I cannot imagine city life without it. There is a quiet **beauty** in riding through the streets in the early morning, when the city is just waking up."},"11":{"w":["blind","blood","blossom","board","bond","bone","boost","border","born","boss","bottom","bound","boundary","bowl","brain","branch","brand","brave","breathe","breed"],"title":"Dogs That Guide the Blind","t":"On the edge of the city, surrounded by fruit trees in **blossom**, lies a training centre for guide dogs. Each year, it prepares dozens of dogs to help **blind** people live independently. Last month, I spent a day there as a volunteer.\n\nThe centre's **boss**, Mrs. Lin, explained that only certain dogs are suitable. The centre chooses each **breed** carefully; Labradors are popular because they are calm and clever. Puppies are **born** at the centre and live with volunteer families for their first year. Vets check their **blood**, **bones|bone** and **brain** development regularly to make sure they are healthy.\n\nTraining is long and demanding. Dogs learn to stop at every **border** between pavement and road, to lead their owners around obstacles and to avoid steps. They must be **brave** in busy streets and calm in crowds. Trainers teach them to stay within a safe **boundary** and to recognise when danger is near. A dog that passes every test is **bound** to change someone's life.\n\nThe **bond** between a dog and its owner is remarkable. One blind woman told me that her dog had given her confidence to travel alone for the first time. \"When I walk with him, I can **breathe** freely,\" she said. \"I am not afraid anymore.\" She keeps his food **bowl** next to her bed.\n\nThe centre depends on donations. A pet food **brand** recently agreed to sponsor it, giving funding a major **boost**. The centre's **board** plans to open a new **branch** in another city next year. From the **bottom** of my heart, I admire everyone who works there."},"12":{"w":["brick","brief","brilliant","broad","broadcast","broken","bronze","bubble","budget","burden","burn","burst","bury","business","cafe","calculate","calendar","calm","camera","camp"],"title":"Opening a Student Cafe","t":"In our second year at university, my friends and I decided to open a small **cafe** on campus. We had a **brilliant** idea: a place where students could study, chat and drink good coffee at low prices. Turning the idea into a real **business**, however, was much harder than we expected.\n\nFirst, we had to plan a **budget**. We sat down to **calculate** the costs of rent, equipment and ingredients. The numbers were frightening, and the financial **burden** seemed huge. To save money, we found an old room with **broken** windows and **brick** walls, and we repaired it ourselves. We bought second-hand furniture and a cheap coffee machine.\n\nThings did not always go smoothly. On our first day, a pipe **burst** and flooded the floor. A week later, someone forgot to turn off the oven, and we **burned|burn** a whole tray of cakes. I wanted to **bury** my head in my hands, but my friend stayed **calm**. \"Every business has problems,\" she said. \"We'll fix them.\"\n\nSlowly, the cafe became popular. We used a **broad** range of marketing ideas, from social media to a short video that the campus radio agreed to **broadcast**. We filmed it with a borrowed **camera**. Each month, we held events listed on a **calendar** by the door, such as quiz nights and music evenings. Students loved the warm atmosphere, and the cafe was always full of chatter and the **bubble** of the coffee machine.\n\nAt the end of the year, the university gave us a **bronze** medal in its student business competition. In a **brief** speech, I thanked our customers. This summer, we will run a training **camp** for other students who dream of starting a business."},"13":{"w":["campaign","campus","canal","cancel","cancer","candidate","canteen","capable","capacity","capital","capture","carbon","career","careless","cash","cause","cautious","celebrate","cell","central"],"title":"Running for Student President","t":"This spring, my roommate Anna decided to become a **candidate** for student union president. I agreed to help with her **campaign**, and for six weeks, our lives revolved around posters, speeches and meetings across the **campus**.\n\nAnna's main **cause** was the environment. She wanted to make our university **carbon** neutral within ten years. She proposed installing solar panels, reducing waste in the **canteen** and encouraging students to cycle. \"Our generation must be **capable** of real change,\" she said in her first speech. Her ideas attracted a lot of support.\n\nThe campaign was not easy. Anna had to be **cautious** about promises she could not keep. One rival candidate was **careless** with facts, and Anna spent hours correcting false information. At one point, a debate had to be **canceled|cancel** because the hall had reached full **capacity** and safety rules were broken. We tried to **capture** every speech on video, and our **cell** phones never stopped ringing.\n\nAnna also organised a charity run along the old **canal** to raise money for **cancer** research. Hundreds of students joined, and we collected more than ten thousand yuan in **cash** and online donations. The event became a **central** part of her campaign, showing that she could bring people together.\n\nOn election day, the results were announced in the main hall. Anna won by a large margin, and we stayed up late to **celebrate**. Her victory was more than a personal success; it showed that young people care about the future. She hopes the experience will help her build a **career** in environmental policy, perhaps one day in the **capital**."},"14":{"w":["century","ceremony","certain","certificate","chain","chairman","challenge","champion","chance","channel","chaos","character","characteristic","charge","charity","charming","chart","chase","cheat","cheer"],"title":"The Charity Games","t":"Every autumn, our city holds a sports festival called the Charity Games. For nearly a **century**, it has raised money for local hospitals and schools. This year, I took part for the first time, and it was an experience I will never forget.\n\nThe opening **ceremony** took place in the city stadium. The **chairman** of the organising committee gave a short speech, explaining that every ticket sold goes to **charity**. Then the crowd began to **cheer** as hundreds of athletes marched in. The **charming** old stadium was decorated with flags, and a television **channel** broadcast the event live.\n\nI joined the relay race with my classmates. It was a real **challenge**, since our team included last year's **champion**. Before the race, the organisers reminded everyone of the rules: anyone who tried to **cheat** would be disqualified. A large **chart** on the wall showed each team's times and ranking.\n\nThe race was full of excitement. At the start, there was a moment of **chaos** when two runners fell, but the referees quickly restored order. Our team had little **chance** of winning, but we ran with all our strength. In the final lap, I tried to **chase** the runner ahead of me, and I almost caught her.\n\nWe finished second, and each of us received a **certificate**. I am **certain** that the festival builds community **character**. One **characteristic** of the Games is that everyone is welcome, whatever their ability. As one volunteer in **charge** of the race told me, \"It's not about winning. It's about being part of a **chain** of kindness.\""},"15":{"w":["cheerful","chemical","chemistry","chess","chief","childhood","choice","choose","circle","circumstance","citizen","civil","civilization","claim","clarify","classic","classical","classification","classify","clearly"],"title":"My Grandfather's Two Loves","t":"My grandfather was a **chemistry** teacher for forty years, and he had two great loves: science and **chess**. Many of my happiest **childhood** memories are of sitting beside him in his study, listening to **classical** music while he explained a **chemical** reaction or showed me a clever chess move.\n\nHe was a **cheerful** man with a gift for teaching. He could **clarify** the most difficult ideas using simple examples. To explain **classification**, he once asked me to **classify** the fruit in our kitchen by colour, shape and taste. \"Scientists organise the world so they can understand it,\" he said. He spoke **clearly** and patiently, never making me feel stupid.\n\nHe also believed that science was part of human **civilization**. He told me stories about ancient inventors and how their discoveries changed history. In his view, every **citizen** should understand basic science, because many **civil** decisions, from energy policy to medicine, depend on it. He disliked people who would **claim** things without evidence.\n\nChess taught me important lessons too. My grandfather said that every move is a **choice**, and that we must **choose** wisely according to the **circumstance**. He was the **chief** organiser of a small chess **circle** in our neighbourhood, where old and young played together every weekend.\n\nToday, I study chemistry at university. When I read a **classic** textbook or face a hard problem, I remember his advice: \"Be curious, be patient and never stop asking questions.\" I still play chess, and every game reminds me of him."},"16":{"w":["clever","climate","climb","closely","clothing","club","coach","coal","coast","coat","collaborate","collapse","colleague","collect","collection","collective","color","combination","combine","comfort"],"title":"A Coastal Town Finds a New Future","t":"For a hundred years, the small town of Redcliff on the northern **coast** depended on **coal**. Most men worked in the mines, and the town's economy rose and fell with the price of fuel. When the last mine closed, people feared the town would **collapse**.\n\nToday, Redcliff is changing. Concern about the **climate** has made coal less popular, and the town has found new ways to survive. A group of local residents decided to **collaborate** with the council and start a tourism project. They worked **closely** with designers and historians to turn the old mine into a museum.\n\nThe museum has a remarkable **collection** of objects from mining life: tools, photographs and the heavy **clothing** that miners wore underground. Visitors can try on an old work **coat** and helmet. A **clever** guide explains how families lived and shows how the town's **collective** spirit helped people survive hard times.\n\nThe town has also become popular with outdoor lovers. A local **club** organises walks along the cliffs, and visitors **climb** to the top for spectacular views. A former miner now works as a walking **coach**, teaching young people about safety and nature. He says it is a **combination** of his two great loves: the land and the people.\n\nNot everyone has adapted easily. Some older residents miss the mines and the friendship of each **colleague** they worked beside. But many feel proud of how the town has managed to **combine** history with new opportunities. Children **collect** shells on the beach, and painted houses add **color** to the streets. For many, there is **comfort** in knowing that Redcliff has a future."},"17":{"w":["comfortable","command","comment","commerce","commercial","commit","commitment","common","commonly","communicate","communication","communist","community","compare","comparison","compete","competition","competitive","complain","complaint"],"title":"The Old Market Square","t":"In the centre of a city in eastern Poland lies an old market square. For centuries, it has been the heart of local **commerce**. During the **communist** period, many shops were closed, and people had to queue for basic goods. Today, the square is lively again, but it faces a new challenge.\n\nLast year, a large **commercial** company opened a shopping centre on the edge of the city. It offers low prices and **comfortable** parking, and many customers now shop there. Small traders in the old square began to **complain** that they could not **compete**. One trader made a formal **complaint** to the city council, saying that the **competition** was unfair.\n\nThe council asked residents for their opinions. Many people left a **comment** online, and the debate became heated. In **comparison** with the shopping centre, the old market is more expensive, but it offers fresh, local products and a sense of **community**. Some residents said that it is **common** for small markets to disappear, while others insisted it must be protected.\n\nIn the end, the council made a **commitment** to support the market. It promised to improve parking, organise festivals and help traders **communicate** with customers through social media. Better **communication**, officials said, would help the market become more **competitive**. The council also promised never to **commit** the old square to commercial development.\n\nThe traders were pleased. One elderly woman, who **commonly** sells cheese at the market, said she had worked there for fifty years. \"When you **compare** the two places,\" she said, \"the shopping centre sells things. Our market sells memories.\" Her words seemed to **command** respect from everyone listening."},"18":{"w":["complete","completely","complex","complicated","comprehensive","compromise","concentrate","concentration","concept","concern","conclude","conclusion","concrete","condition","conduct","conference","confidence","confident","confirm","conflict"],"title":"My First Academic Conference","t":"Last spring, I presented my research at an international student **conference** for the first time. My project was about the effect of noise on **concentration** in classrooms. I had spent a year working on it, but I was still nervous about speaking in front of experts.\n\nPreparing was **complicated**. My topic was **complex**, and I had to explain the main **concept** clearly in fifteen minutes. My supervisor helped me **conduct** a final round of tests to **confirm** my results. She also told me to use **concrete** examples, such as real classroom recordings, instead of only numbers.\n\nOn the day of the conference, I tried to **concentrate** on my notes, but my hands were shaking. When I began speaking, however, my **confidence** grew. I explained that students' attention dropped **completely** when noise rose above a certain level, and that poor classroom **conditions|condition** were a serious **concern** in many schools.\n\nAfter my talk, a professor raised a question that led to a small **conflict**. He felt my data was not **comprehensive** enough, since I had studied only three schools. We discussed the issue politely and reached a **compromise**: I would include more schools in the next stage of the project. I felt more **confident** after the discussion, not less.\n\nAt the end of the conference, the organisers asked each speaker to **conclude** with one key message. Mine was simple: quiet classrooms help students learn. I was proud to **complete** my first conference, and my **conclusion** was that sharing research is just as important as doing it."},"19":{"w":["congress","connect","connection","conscious","consciousness","consequence","consequently","conservation","conserve","consider","considerable","consideration","consist","consistent","constant","construct","construction","consult","consume","consumer"],"title":"Saving Water, Saving Energy","t":"In many cities, people **consume** far more water and energy than they need. They leave lights on, take long showers and buy products that waste resources. Experts warn that the **consequence** of this behaviour is serious: shortages, higher prices and environmental damage. **Consequently**, many governments now promote **conservation**.\n\nChanging habits is not easy. Most people are not **conscious** of how much water they use every day. A single shower can use more than fifty litres. To raise **consciousness**, some cities send monthly reports showing how each household's use compares with its neighbours'. Studies show that this simple **connection** to others can reduce waste by a **considerable** amount.\n\nExperts also **consider** the role of design. New buildings can be designed to **conserve** energy, with good insulation and solar panels. Many builders now **construct** homes that collect rainwater, and green **construction** has become a fast-growing industry. Each new building should **consist** of materials that are safe, durable and recyclable.\n\nGovernments play an important part. Last year, the national **congress** passed a law requiring companies to show energy use on labels, so that every **consumer** can make informed choices. Before buying a fridge or washing machine, shoppers can **consult** the label and choose the most efficient model. Policies must be **consistent** and **constant** if they are to change behaviour.\n\nIndividuals can help too. We can **connect** saving resources with saving money, which gives people a strong reason to change. Showing **consideration** for the planet does not require huge sacrifices. Turning off a tap or a light is a small step, but if millions of people do it, the effect is enormous."},"20":{"w":["consumption","contact","contain","contemporary","content","continue","contract","contrary","contrast","contribute","contribution","control","convenience","convenient","conventional","conversely","convert","convey","convince","cooperate"],"title":"Shopping Online or in Stores?","t":"Online shopping has become a major part of **contemporary** life. With a few clicks, consumers can buy almost anything and have it delivered to their door. The **convenience** is obvious, and many people find it more **convenient** than visiting a shop. Yet traditional stores **continue** to survive, and the debate about which is better is far from over.\n\nSupporters of online shopping point to its low prices and wide choice. Online stores **contain** millions of products, and customers can easily **contrast** prices and read reviews. **Conversely**, critics argue that online shopping increases **consumption**, encouraging people to buy things they do not need. Packaging waste also **contributes|contribute** to pollution.\n\n**Conventional** shops have their own advantages. Customers can touch products, try on clothes and **contact** staff directly. Shop assistants can **convey** information that a website cannot, such as how a jacket really feels. Some shops have even started to **convert** part of their space into cafés, hoping to attract customers who want a social experience.\n\nMany experts believe that the two forms of shopping can **cooperate** rather than compete. Some companies sign a **contract** with local shops so that customers can order online and collect in store. This makes a positive **contribution** to local economies and helps businesses **control** costs. Some people assume that physical shops are no longer needed. On the **contrary**, closing them all would seriously harm city centres.\n\nIn my view, both have their place. Online shopping saves time, but shops offer human contact. Before buying, consumers should consider the **content** of the product description carefully. A good balance will **convince** more people that shopping can be both efficient and responsible."},"21":{"w":["coordinate","cope","core","corporation","correct","corresponding","costly","count","county","couple","courage","course","craft","create","creative","crime","criminal","crisis","critical","criticism"],"title":"A County Fights Back","t":"Ten years ago, a rural **county** in the north of England faced a serious **crisis**. Its largest employer, a steel **corporation**, closed its factory, and two thousand people lost their jobs. Unemployment rose, young people left, and the local **crime** rate began to climb. Many residents wondered how they would **cope**.\n\nThe local council decided to act. It brought together businesses, schools and charities to **coordinate** a recovery plan. At its **core** was a simple idea: help people **create** their own jobs. The council offered training **courses|course** in business, design and traditional **craft** skills, from woodwork to pottery. A **corresponding** fund gave small loans to new businesses.\n\nThe plan was not without **criticism**. Some residents argued that it was too **costly** and that the money should go to the police instead. Others said that only a large company could save the county. These debates were **critical** to the project's development, forcing leaders to explain their plans clearly and **correct** their mistakes.\n\nSlowly, the results began to show. A young **couple** opened a furniture workshop that now employs twelve people. A former steelworker started a **creative** design studio. The police reported that fewer young people were becoming involved in crime, and the number of **criminal** cases fell by a third. Every new job, officials said, would **count**.\n\nToday, the county is not rich, but it is hopeful. The mayor praised residents for their **courage** during the hardest years. \"We lost a factory,\" he said, \"but we found ourselves.\""},"22":{"w":["criticize","crop","cross","crowd","crucial","cultivate","culture","cure","curiosity","curious","current","custom","customer","cycle","daily","dam","damage","danger","dangerous","data"],"title":"The Dam and the Farmers","t":"For centuries, farmers in a river valley in southwest China have grown rice and vegetables on the fertile land. Their **culture** and **customs|custom** are closely tied to the river. When the government announced plans to build a **dam** across the valley, the news caused a heated debate.\n\nSupporters said the dam was **crucial** for the region's future. It would produce clean electricity and help control floods, which often **damage** homes and destroy each year's **crop**. Engineers presented **data** showing that floods had become more **dangerous** in recent decades. Without action, they argued, the **danger** would only grow.\n\nSome villagers, however, began to **criticize** the plan. They feared that the dam would flood their land and force them to leave. They also worried that it would change the river's natural **cycle**, affecting fish and soil. Older farmers, who still **cultivate** the land by hand, said that no amount of money could **cure** the pain of losing their homes.\n\nScientists tried to answer people's questions. Many villagers were **curious** about how the dam would work, and their **curiosity** led to public meetings where engineers explained the design. The **current** plan includes a new village for families who must move, and farmland on higher ground.\n\nThe debate continues. On market days, a **crowd** gathers in the village square to discuss the latest news. Some people **cross** the river to sell vegetables to every **customer** in town, as their parents did. For them, the river is part of **daily** life, and change will not be easy."},"23":{"w":["deal","death","debate","debt","decade","decent","decision","declare","decline","decorate","decrease","deepen","defeat","defend","define","definitely","definition","degree","delay","deliberately"],"title":"Should University Be Free?","t":"Over the past **decade**, the cost of university education has risen sharply in many countries. Students now graduate with large amounts of **debt**, and many worry about paying it back. The question of whether university should be free has become a major public **debate**.\n\nThose in favour argue that education is a basic right. A university **degree** can **deepen** a person's knowledge and improve their chances of finding a **decent** job. When students are burdened with loans, they may **delay** important life choices, such as buying a home or starting a family. Supporters **declare** that free education would benefit society as a whole.\n\nOthers **defend** the current system. They argue that free education would be expensive for taxpayers and could **decrease** the quality of teaching. Some politicians **deliberately** avoid the issue, knowing it divides voters. A recent proposal to remove tuition fees suffered a narrow **defeat** in parliament, though supporters say they will try again.\n\nExperts point out that the **definition** of \"free\" can vary. In some countries, tuition is free but living costs are not. It is important to **define** clearly what support students need. Data show that student numbers **decline** when fees rise, especially among poorer families.\n\nIn my view, governments should **definitely** reduce the financial burden on students. A fair **deal** might combine low fees with grants for those in need. This **decision** will shape the future of a whole generation. Families still proudly **decorate** their walls with their children's graduation photos; no student should have to fear that such a moment means years of debt. A society that ignores this problem risks the slow **death** of opportunity."},"24":{"w":["deliver","delivery","demand","demonstrate","dense","density","deny","department","depend","dependent","depression","derive","describe","description","desert","deserve","design","desirable","desire","desperate"],"title":"Life as a Delivery Rider","t":"In China's big cities, food **delivery** riders are everywhere. Wearing bright jackets, they ride electric scooters through **dense** traffic, carrying meals to offices and homes. The **demand** for their services has grown rapidly, and millions of people now **depend** on them every day.\n\nThe job is hard. Riders must **deliver** each order within thirty minutes, and the high population **density** of cities means that roads are often crowded. One rider, Mr. Zhang, described his typical day: twelve hours on the road, in heat, rain and cold. \"Customers **desire** speed above everything,\" he said. \"Sometimes I feel **desperate** when I am late.\"\n\nMany riders are **dependent** on the job to support their families. They **derive** most of their income from tips and bonuses, so a single bad review can hurt. Researchers have found that the stress can lead to **depression**, and some riders say they feel lonely despite meeting hundreds of people a day. Mr. Zhang's own **description** of the work was simple: \"Fast, tiring and invisible.\"\n\nCompanies **deny** that riders are treated unfairly, and they **demonstrate** new safety measures, such as better insurance. However, critics say that the **design** of the delivery apps puts too much pressure on workers. A government **department** recently announced new rules limiting working hours, a change many consider **desirable**.\n\nRiders **deserve** respect. They make city life more convenient, often at great cost to themselves. Mr. Zhang dreams of one day opening a small restaurant in his hometown, near the edge of the **desert** in Gansu. \"Then people will **describe** my food,\" he said with a smile, \"not just my speed.\""},"25":{"w":["despite","destination","destroy","destruction","destructive","detail","detect","determination","determine","device","devote","dialect","diet","differ","difference","difficulty","digital","diligent","dimension","direct"],"title":"Saving Disappearing Dialects","t":"China has hundreds of local dialects, but many are slowly disappearing. Young people increasingly speak standard Mandarin, and some **dialects|dialect** are now used only by elderly villagers. Linguists warn that the loss of a dialect can **destroy** an important part of local culture.\n\n**Despite** these challenges, a group of university students has found a new way to help. They travel to remote villages with a simple **digital** recording **device** and ask old people to tell stories, sing songs and describe local customs. Every recording is carefully labelled with **details|detail** about the speaker and place.\n\nThe work requires great **determination**. Some villages are difficult to reach, and many elderly speakers are shy at first. The students must be **diligent** and patient. Their **difficulty** is not only technical: they must **determine** which recordings are most valuable and **detect** small differences in pronunciation. Even neighbouring villages may **differ** in the way they say common words.\n\nThe project has revealed a new **dimension** of local history. Old songs describe floods, migrations and festivals, preserving memories that might otherwise disappear. Speakers also describe the traditional local **diet**, from pickled vegetables to home-made rice wine. One elderly woman said that speaking her dialect made her feel young again. The students say that this human connection is the most important **difference** their work makes.\n\nThe loss of dialects is not always caused by **destructive** forces; it is often the natural result of change. But the **destruction** of cultural memory is real. The students **devote** their weekends to the project, and they plan to share the recordings online so that anyone can listen. Their next **destination** is a mountain village in Guizhou, where only thirty people still speak the local dialect. The recordings, they hope, will give young villagers a **direct** link to their grandparents' voices."},"26":{"w":["direction","directly","disadvantage","disagree","disappear","disappoint","disaster","discipline","disclose","discount","discourage","discover","discovery","discrimination","discussion","disease","dismiss","disorder","display","disposal"],"title":"When a Company Hides the Truth","t":"Last year, a large food company found itself at the centre of a public **disaster**. Several customers became ill after eating its frozen dumplings, and doctors traced the **disease** to bacteria in a factory. Investigators later made a shocking **discovery**: the company had known about the problem for weeks but had chosen not to **disclose** it.\n\nThe news caused a heated public **discussion**. Many people were deeply **disappointed|disappoint**, saying they had trusted the brand for years. Others pointed out that the company's poor **discipline** in food safety was part of a wider **disorder** in the industry. Some experts warned that, without stricter rules, similar problems would not **disappear**.\n\nThe company tried to repair its reputation. It offered a large **discount** on other products and put new safety notices on **display** in every shop. Critics **dismissed|dismiss** these efforts as public relations. They demanded better waste **disposal** in factories and regular inspections. A few people even began to **discourage** friends from buying any frozen food at all.\n\nThe scandal also raised questions about workers' rights. Several employees said they had been told not to speak **directly** to journalists. One worker, who had tried to warn managers, said she was later treated unfairly, a form of **discrimination** she believed was meant to silence her. Not everyone agreed with her account, and the company continues to **disagree** with her claims.\n\nThe case has pushed the industry in a new **direction**. Companies now **discover** that hiding problems is a serious **disadvantage**: in the age of social media, the truth always comes out. Honesty, experts say, is not only right but also good business."},"27":{"w":["dispose","dispute","distance","distant","distinguish","distribute","distribution","district","disturb","diverse","divide","document","domestic","dominate","doubt","draft","dragon","drain","drama","dramatic"],"title":"The Festival That Divided a District","t":"Every summer, an old **district** in the south of our city holds a lively **dragon** festival. For three days, the streets are filled with music, food stalls and **drama** performances. A huge dragon made of silk dances through the narrow lanes, and the atmosphere is truly **dramatic**.\n\nIn recent years, however, the festival has caused a **dispute**. Some new residents complained that the noise would **disturb** their sleep and that the crowds made it hard to get home. They wanted the festival moved to a park at a safe **distance** from people's houses. Long-time residents strongly disagreed, saying the festival is part of the district's identity.\n\nThe council tried to **divide** the problem into smaller issues. It held meetings and asked both sides to help **draft** new rules. A final **document** set limits on noise after ten o'clock and required organisers to **dispose** of rubbish properly. Volunteers agreed to **distribute** leaflets explaining the changes, and the council improved the **distribution** of bins along the streets.\n\nSome people **doubt** whether the compromise will work. Big festivals can **drain** a district's budget, and a few critics fear that commercial stalls will **dominate** the event. Others say the festival attracts **domestic** tourists from **distant** cities, bringing money to local shops.\n\nThe festival today is more **diverse** than ever, with performances from many cultures. As one organiser said, \"We must **distinguish** between tradition and noise. The dragon belongs to everyone.\""},"28":{"w":["drawing","dream","drop","drought","drug","due","dull","durable","duration","duty","dynamic","eager","earn","earthquake","ease","easily","economic","economy","edge","educate"],"title":"Rebuilding After the Earthquake","t":"Five years ago, a strong **earthquake** struck a mountain village in western China, destroying most of its houses. Before the village could recover, a long **drought** followed, and crops failed for two years. Many families lost not only their homes but also their way to **earn** a living.\n\nRebuilding was a long process. Engineers designed new houses that were **durable** and able to withstand future earthquakes. Volunteers helped clear the ruins, and doctors brought medicine and medical **drug** supplies for the injured. Officials said it was their **duty** to make sure no family was left behind.\n\nThe local **economy** slowly recovered. The government offered **economic** support to farmers, and new roads were built along the **edge** of the mountains, making it easier to sell vegetables in town. Prices did not **drop** as much as people feared, and tourism began to grow. The recovery took less time than anyone expected; officials said its short **duration** was **due** largely to the hard work of villagers themselves.\n\nChildren were especially affected. To **ease** their fear, teachers organised **drawing** classes where children could express their feelings. One girl drew a house with a red roof and said it was her **dream** home. Teachers believe that the best way to help children recover is to **educate** them and give them hope.\n\nToday, the village is **dynamic** and full of life. Young people are **eager** to stay and build businesses. Life is still not always **easily** managed, and some days can feel **dull** and hard, but the villagers are proud of what they have rebuilt together."},"29":{"w":["education","effect","effective","efficiency","efficient","effort","elaborate","elderly","election","electric","electricity","electronic","element","elementary","eliminate","embrace","emerge","emergency","emit","emotion"],"title":"Electric Buses and Older Passengers","t":"Last year, our city replaced its old diesel buses with **electric** ones. The change was part of a promise made by the mayor during her **election** campaign. She said that cleaner transport was a necessary step in protecting the environment.\n\nThe new buses are quiet and **efficient**. They do not **emit** harmful gases, and they run on **electricity** generated from wind and solar power. Engineers say the buses have greatly improved the **efficiency** of public transport. An **electronic** screen in each bus shows the next stop, and passengers can pay with their phones.\n\nThe change has had a positive **effect** on air quality, but it has also created challenges. Many **elderly** passengers found the new payment system confusing. Some felt strong **emotion** when the old buses disappeared, since they had used them for decades. The city responded by offering free classes to help older people **embrace** new technology.\n\nThese classes have proved **effective**. Volunteers explain the **elementary** steps of using a phone to pay, and they use simple language to **eliminate** confusion. One **element** of the programme that people especially like is the patient, friendly attitude of the volunteers. The city has also introduced an **emergency** button on every bus for passengers who need help.\n\nOfficials say the project shows the importance of **education** as well as technology. It took a great deal of **effort** to plan the change, and the city produced an **elaborate** guide for every resident. New problems will certainly **emerge**, but most people agree that the city is moving in the right direction."},"30":{"w":["emotional","emperor","emphasis","emphasize","employ","employee","employer","employment","enable","enclose","encounter","encourage","endure","energy","enforce","engage","engineer","engineering","enhance","enormous"],"title":"Finding Work as a Young Engineer","t":"Finding **employment** after university can be an **emotional** experience. Last summer, my cousin Lin graduated with a degree in **engineering**, and she began searching for a job. She discovered that the job market was competitive, and she had to **endure** many rejections before finding the right position.\n\nLin learned a lot from the process. Many companies **employ** hundreds of new graduates each year, but they look for more than good grades. One **employer** told her that the **emphasis** is now on teamwork and problem-solving. Interviewers would often **emphasize** practical experience, asking about projects she had completed at university.\n\nLin applied for dozens of jobs. With each application, she would **enclose** a short letter explaining why she wanted the position. During one interview, she had to **encounter** a difficult question: \"Describe a time when you failed.\" She answered honestly, and the manager later said her honesty was impressive.\n\nFinally, she found a job as an **engineer** at a company that designs clean **energy** systems. The company's buildings use **enormous** solar panels, and new **employees|employee** receive training to **enhance** their skills. Her manager believes that a good workplace should **enable** people to grow and **engage** with meaningful work.\n\nLin's company also takes safety seriously. It has strict rules, and managers **enforce** them carefully. Lin says her parents always **encouraged|encourage** her to follow her interests. She now gives talks to students, telling them that one day they might design the cities of the future, just as ancient builders once designed palaces for an **emperor**."},"31":{"w":["enroll","ensure","entertainment","enthusiasm","enthusiastic","entire","environment","equal","equality","equip","equipment","era","escape","especially","essay","essential","establish","estate","estimate","evaluate"],"title":"Girls Who Build Robots","t":"Three years ago, a secondary school in our city decided to **establish** a robotics club for girls. At the time, very few girls took part in engineering activities, and teachers wanted to **ensure** that everyone had an **equal** chance to learn. Today, the club has more than sixty members, and its success has inspired schools across the country.\n\nThe club began with almost nothing. There was no money for **equipment**, and the teachers had to **estimate** costs carefully. A local **estate** agent donated a small office, and a technology company offered to **equip** it with computers and tools. The **entire** project depended on volunteers.\n\nThe students' **enthusiasm** surprised everyone. They stayed after school to build robots, and they were **enthusiastic** about entering national competitions. Their teachers say this kind of hands-on learning is **essential** for developing problem-solving skills. **Especially** important, they add, is the confidence that comes from building something that works.\n\nThe club also promotes gender **equality**. Many girls said they had once believed engineering was \"for boys\". Now several plan to **enroll** in engineering courses at university. One student wrote an **essay** about her experience, describing how the club helped her **escape** the idea that she was not good at maths.\n\nExperts who **evaluate** the programme say it creates a positive learning **environment**. It is also fun: the club organises robot races as **entertainment** for younger pupils. As one teacher said, \"We are entering a new **era**, and our girls are ready for it.\""},"32":{"w":["event","eventually","everyday","evidence","evident","evolution","evolve","exactly","exaggerate","examine","exceed","excellent","exception","excessive","exchange","exclude","exclusive","exhaust","exhaustion","exhibition"],"title":"The Evolution Exhibition","t":"Last month, the city science museum opened a new **exhibition** on human **evolution**. It explains how humans have changed over millions of years and how we continue to **evolve** today. The exhibition has become the museum's most popular **event** of the year.\n\nVisitors begin by examining fossils and stone tools. Scientists **examine** these objects to find **evidence** about how early humans lived. It is **evident** from the displays that our ancestors were clever and adaptable. A guide told us that we do not know **exactly** when humans first used fire, but the **evidence** suggests it was at least a million years ago.\n\nThe exhibition also connects the past with **everyday** life. One section shows how modern diets, with their **excessive** sugar, are very different from the foods our bodies evolved to eat. Another explains how cultural **exchange** has shaped human history. The curators are careful not to **exaggerate** or simplify complex science; every claim is supported by research.\n\nSome special objects are on **exclusive** loan from museums abroad, and the number of visitors has already begun to **exceed** expectations. The museum had to extend its opening hours. Staff told us they worked to the point of **exhaustion** during the first week. \"The crowds **exhaust** us,\" one guide said with a smile, \"but in the best way.\"\n\nThe exhibition does not **exclude** anyone: there are guides in five languages and activities for children. With very few **exceptions|exception**, visitors leave impressed. Critics have described it as **excellent**, and many say it **eventually** changed the way they think about being human."},"33":{"w":["exist","existence","expand","expansion","expectation","expense","experience","experiment","expert","explain","explanation","explore","explosion","export","expose","exposure","express","expression","extend","extensive"],"title":"Exploring the Universe","t":"For thousands of years, people have looked at the night sky and wondered about our **existence**. Today, scientists **explore** the universe using powerful telescopes and space missions. Their discoveries continue to **expand** our understanding of where we come from.\n\nOne of the most exciting areas of research is the **expansion** of the universe. Scientists have shown that galaxies are moving away from each other, and that the universe began with a huge **explosion** called the Big Bang. Experts **explain** that this event happened about fourteen billion years ago. Their **explanation** is supported by **extensive** evidence, including light from the early universe.\n\nAnother big question is whether life might **exist** on other planets. To find out, researchers carry out each **experiment** with great care. They study how living things survive **exposure** to extreme heat, cold and radiation. When scientists **expose** tiny organisms to such conditions and some survive, it suggests that life might be possible elsewhere.\n\nSpace research is expensive, and some people question the **expense**. Yet supporters argue that the benefits **extend** far beyond science. Technology developed for space, such as satellites and new materials, is now a major **export** for many countries. Space missions also inspire young people and help them **express** their curiosity.\n\nMany astronauts describe their **experience** of seeing Earth from space as life-changing. One used a simple **expression** to describe it: \"Our planet looks small and fragile.\" An **expert** on space history said that public **expectation** of new discoveries has never been higher. The universe, it seems, still has many secrets to share."},"34":{"w":["extent","extra","extraordinary","extreme","extremely","facility","fact","factor","failure","fair","faith","familiar","farmer","fashion","fatigue","favor","favorable","fear","feasible","feature"],"title":"Farmers Facing Extreme Weather","t":"Climate change is affecting farmers around the world. Weather has become more **extreme**, with longer droughts, stronger storms and unexpected frosts. For a **farmer**, these changes can mean the difference between success and **failure**.\n\nMr. Li has grown vegetables in his village for thirty years. He says the weather today is **extremely** unpredictable. \"Things I was once **familiar** with have changed completely,\" he explains. Last summer, a heatwave destroyed half his crops, and he worked until he was overcome by **fatigue**. He admits that **fear** of the next disaster is always with him.\n\nExperts say that several **factors|factor** explain these problems. The most important is rising temperatures, but poor soil and limited water also play a role. To some **extent**, farmers can adapt by changing what they grow and when they plant. One new **feature** of farming in the region is the use of greenhouses, which protect crops from bad weather.\n\nThe government has built a new irrigation **facility** in each village and offers **extra** support to farmers who try new methods. Officials say these measures are **feasible** and will have a **favorable** effect on harvests. Many farmers believe the support is **fair**, though some complain that big farms receive more help. It is a **fact** that small farmers are often the most vulnerable.\n\nDespite the challenges, Mr. Li has not lost **faith** in the land. He has started growing organic vegetables, which have become something of a **fashion** among city shoppers. A local supermarket has done him a big **favor** by selling his produce. \"Farming has always been hard,\" he says. \"But with **extraordinary** effort, we can survive.\""},"35":{"w":["fee","feedback","feeling","female","fertile","festival","fiction","fierce","fight","figure","final","finally","finance","financial","finding","firm","fix","flat","flexible","flood"],"title":"From Finance to Farming","t":"For five years, Zhang Wei worked for a large **financial** **firm** in Shanghai. She earned a good salary and lived in a modern **flat**, but she was unhappy. \"I spent my days looking at **figures|figure** on a screen,\" she says. \"I had no **feeling** of doing anything meaningful.\"\n\nTwo years ago, she made a bold decision. She left her job in **finance** and moved to her grandparents' village, where the land is **fertile** and the air is clean. She started a small organic farm, using her savings to **fix** an old farmhouse. Friends thought she was crazy, but she was determined.\n\nThe first year was hard. A **flood** destroyed part of her crops, and she had to **fight** to keep the business alive. Competition from large farms was **fierce**. But Zhang listened carefully to **feedback** from customers and adjusted her methods. Her **finding** was simple: people wanted fresh vegetables, delivered quickly. She created a **flexible** delivery service, charging only a small **fee**.\n\nZhang is one of a growing number of **female** entrepreneurs in rural China. She says that women bring new ideas to farming, and she now trains other young women to start businesses. Each year, she organises a harvest **festival** where villagers and city visitors can meet, eat and celebrate together.\n\nIn her spare time, Zhang writes **fiction** about village life, and she hopes to publish her first novel soon. Her **final** goal is to show that rural life can be modern and rewarding. \"I **finally** feel that my life has a purpose,\" she says."},"36":{"w":["flourish","flow","fluent","focus","folk","follow","following","fond","forbid","force","forecast","foreigner","forest","forever","forgive","formal","formation","former","fortunate","fortunately"],"title":"A Folk Music Festival in the Forest","t":"Every summer, a small town in the mountains of Yunnan holds a **folk** music festival in the **forest** outside the town. Musicians from many ethnic groups perform traditional songs, and thousands of visitors come to listen. The festival has helped local culture **flourish**.\n\nThis year, I attended with my friend Tom, a **foreigner** from Canada who speaks **fluent** Chinese. He is **fond** of traditional music and has studied it for years. \"Folk songs are a window into people's lives,\" he told me. \"They show how people love, work and **forgive**.\"\n\nThe weather **forecast** had predicted rain, but **fortunately** the sky stayed clear. Music seemed to **flow** through the trees, and the crowd followed the musicians from one stage to another. In the **following** hours, we heard love songs, work songs and songs about the **former** lives of mountain villagers. Some performers wore **formal** costumes, while others played in everyday clothes.\n\nThe organisers have strict rules to protect the forest. They **forbid** plastic bottles and ask visitors to **follow** marked paths. Volunteers never **force** anyone to obey; they rely on signs and friendly reminders instead. The festival's main **focus**, they say, is to celebrate culture without harming nature.\n\nThe festival began twenty years ago with just a handful of musicians. Its **formation** was the idea of a local teacher who feared that young people would forget their traditions. Today, she feels **fortunate** to see children singing the songs she loves. \"Music can live **forever**,\" she said, \"if we keep singing it.\""},"37":{"w":["fortune","found","foundation","fragment","framework","frank","freedom","freely","frequency","frequent","frequently","fresh","frontier","fruitful","frustrate","fuel","function","fund","fundamental","furniture"],"title":"A Second Life for Old Furniture","t":"Every year, millions of pieces of **furniture** are thrown away, even though many are still in good condition. Five years ago, a young engineer named Chen Yu decided to **found** a charity to solve this problem. Today, her **foundation** collects unwanted furniture, repairs it and gives it to families in need.\n\nThe idea came from a **frustrating|frustrate** experience. When Chen moved flats, she wanted to donate her old sofa, but no organisation would take it. \"It seemed wasteful to throw away something so useful,\" she says. She started a small project with her own savings and a **fund** raised from friends.\n\nThe charity's success depends on a clear **framework**. Volunteers check each piece of furniture, repair small faults and give it a **fresh** coat of paint. Even a broken chair can be rebuilt from a **fragment** of wood and new parts. Every item must **function** safely before it is delivered. Chen is **frank** about the challenges: \"Transport is expensive, and **fuel** costs keep rising.\"\n\nThe work has been **fruitful**. The charity now delivers furniture to more than five hundred families a year, and the **frequency** of requests continues to grow. Chen says that her volunteers **frequently** receive letters of thanks. For many families, a bed or a table gives them the **freedom** to live with dignity.\n\nChen believes that reuse is a **fundamental** part of a sustainable future. She speaks **freely** about her mission at schools and conferences, and she is a **frequent** guest on local radio. Asked whether she regrets leaving engineering, which might have made her a **fortune**, she smiles. \"I am working at a new **frontier**,\" she says. \"The frontier of kindness.\""},"38":{"w":["further","furthermore","gain","gallery","gap","garbage","gas","gather","gene","general","generally","generate","generation","generous","genetic","genius","gentle","gently","genuine","geography"],"title":"Art from Garbage","t":"Last month, a small **gallery** in Beijing opened an unusual exhibition. Every artwork was made from **garbage**: plastic bottles, old phones, broken toys and empty **gas** cans. The artist, a young woman named Liu Mei, wanted to show how much waste our **generation** produces.\n\nLiu spent a year collecting materials. She would **gather** rubbish from streets, beaches and recycling centres. Her most famous piece, a giant whale made of plastic bottles, attracted thousands of visitors. Critics praised her as a **genius**, though she insists she is simply a **genuine** environmentalist. \"I want people to see the problem with their own eyes,\" she says.\n\nThe exhibition also highlights a **gap** between generations. Many older visitors said that, in their youth, people **generally** repaired things instead of throwing them away. Younger visitors admitted they rarely think about waste. Liu hopes her work will help close this gap. **Furthermore**, she believes that art can **generate** real change by touching people's emotions.\n\nThe exhibition includes educational displays. One explains how plastic waste travels across oceans, using maps to show the **geography** of pollution. Another describes scientific research on how chemicals can affect a **gene**, raising questions about **genetic** damage in sea animals. In **general**, the message is clear: our waste does not disappear.\n\nVisitors were moved. One elderly man **gently** touched the plastic whale and said it reminded him of the sea where he grew up. A **generous** local company donated money so that schools could visit for free. Liu plans to take the exhibition **further**, touring other cities next year. She hopes people will **gain** a new respect for the planet, and she speaks in a soft, **gentle** voice about the future."},"39":{"w":["gesture","giant","glance","global","globe","glorious","glory","goal","gold","golden","goods","govern","government","gradual","gradually","graduate","grain","grand","grant","graph"],"title":"Feeding the World","t":"Food security is one of the **global** challenges of our time. As the world's population grows, countries must produce more **grain** to feed their people. Last year, my cousin, a university **graduate** in economics, began research on how grain prices affect families around the **globe**.\n\nHer work begins with data. Using a **graph**, she showed me how prices of rice and wheat have risen and fallen over the past thirty years. Changes in weather, trade and fuel costs all play a role. Prices have increased **gradually** in most years, but sudden jumps can cause serious problems for poor families. Some economists compare the market to a **giant** machine that is hard to control.\n\nShe explained that every **government** must balance different needs. Leaders who **govern** large countries must protect farmers while keeping food affordable for city residents. Some governments **grant** money to farmers, while others control the price of basic **goods**. These policies have helped many countries move from hunger to stability, a change she describes as a **glorious** achievement of the past century.\n\nChina, she noted, has made **gradual** progress towards its **goal** of food security. It now feeds nearly a fifth of the world's population. Farmers in the north harvest **golden** fields of wheat each summer, and new technology has improved yields. With a quick **glance** at her charts, she could show how much production has grown.\n\nMy cousin hopes to work for an international organisation. She says there is no **glory** in being rich while others go hungry. Last month, she received a research award, a small **gold** medal presented at a **grand** ceremony. She accepted it with a modest **gesture** of thanks."},"40":{"w":["grasp","grateful","gratitude","gravity","greatly","greedy","greenhouse","greet","grocery","growth","guarantee","guard","guidance","guide","guilty","gunpowder","habit","halt","handful","handle"],"title":"The Greenhouse Grocery","t":"On the edge of our town, a family runs a small **grocery** shop with a difference: almost everything it sells is grown in its own **greenhouse**. The shop has become very popular, and its **growth** over the past five years has **greatly** surprised the owners.\n\nThe owner, Mr. Huang, used to work as a security **guard** at a factory. When the factory had to **halt** production, he lost his job. Instead of looking for similar work, he decided to grow vegetables. \"I had only a **handful** of seeds and a small piece of land,\" he says. Local farmers gave him **guidance**, and he learned quickly.\n\nMr. Huang works hard. Every morning, he opens the shop and comes out to **greet** customers by name. He can **guarantee** that his vegetables are fresh, because they are picked the same day. He has a **habit** of giving children a free tomato when they visit. \"You have to **handle** every plant with care,\" he tells them, \"and every customer too.\"\n\nMr. Huang is **grateful** for the support of his community. He often expresses his **gratitude** by donating vegetables to elderly neighbours. He refuses to be **greedy**, keeping his prices low even when demand is high. \"I would feel **guilty** if poor families could not afford healthy food,\" he says.\n\nHe also enjoys teaching. He sometimes acts as a **guide** for school groups, explaining how plants grow towards the light and how **gravity** helps roots grow downwards. Once a year, he takes students to a nearby museum about ancient Chinese inventions, from paper to **gunpowder**. He wants them to **grasp** an important idea: great things often start from small beginnings."},"41":{"w":["hang","happen","happiness","hardly","hardship","hardware","harm","harmful","harmony","harness","harsh","harvest","hazard","headache","headline","headquarters","heal","heat","heavily","height"],"title":"Sunlight on the Farm","t":"Last summer, an extreme **heat** wave hit northern China. Temperatures reached record **heights|height**, and many farmers faced **hardship** as crops dried in the fields. In some areas, the **harvest** was cut by half. Newspapers ran **headline** after headline about the crisis.\n\nIn one village, however, farmers had prepared for such a **hazard**. Two years earlier, they had decided to **harness** the power of the sun. With support from an energy company whose **headquarters** is in Beijing, they installed solar panels above their fields. The panels provide shade for crops and generate electricity at the same time.\n\nThe idea sounded strange at first. Some villagers worried that the panels would **harm** the plants or that the **hardware** would be too expensive to maintain. But the results have been impressive. Shaded crops suffer less in **harsh** sunlight, and the electricity is sold to the national grid, giving farmers extra income.\n\nThe project has brought more than money. Farmers say they no longer fear the summer. \"Before, a heatwave was a real **headache**,\" one farmer said. \"Now we can **hardly** believe how different things are.\" The panels also reduce the need for **harmful** fuels, helping to protect the environment. Villagers describe a new sense of **harmony** between farming and technology.\n\nExperts say such projects will become common. Climate change may **happen** faster than expected, and farmers must adapt. A local official said the project had helped the village **heal** after years of poor harvests, and that it depends **heavily** on cooperation between farmers and companies. Today, a photo of the solar fields proudly **hangs|hang** in the village hall, a symbol of the **happiness** the project has brought."},"42":{"w":["helpful","helpless","hence","hero","hesitate","hide","highlight","highly","highway","hint","hire","historical","history","hit","hole","honest","honesty","hopeful","hopeless","horizon"],"title":"An Ordinary Hero","t":"Late one winter night, a lorry driver named Wang Jun was driving along a **highway** in the mountains when he saw smoke rising from a car that had crashed into a ditch. He did not **hesitate** for a second: he stopped his lorry and ran to help. The story soon made him a national **hero**.\n\nInside the car, a young mother and her child were trapped and **helpless**. The doors would not open, and smoke was pouring from a **hole** in the engine. Wang used a tool to **hit** the window until it broke. He pulled the woman and child out just before the car caught fire.\n\nWhen journalists asked him about the rescue, Wang refused to **hide** behind modest phrases. \"I was scared,\" he admitted with typical **honesty**. \"But I knew that if I waited, it would be **hopeless**.\" His **honest** answer touched many people. He said he only did what any person would do, and **hence** he did not deserve special praise.\n\nThe story was widely shared online, and newspapers chose to **highlight** it as an example of courage. A transport company offered to **hire** him as a safety trainer, and the mother wrote him a letter saying he had given her family a **hopeful** future. Experts on road safety used the case to give drivers a useful **hint**: always carry a tool that can break glass.\n\nWang is **highly** respected in his hometown now, but he continues to drive his lorry. He says that the open road gives him a wide **horizon** and time to think. His grandfather, a soldier, often told him stories from **history** about brave ordinary people. \"Real **historical** heroes were not famous,\" Wang said. \"They were simply **helpful** when it mattered.\""},"43":{"w":["horrible","horror","host","hostile","household","however","huge","human","humble","humid","humor","hunger","hunt","hurt","ideal","identical","identify","identity","idiom","ignore"],"title":"Twins in the Kitchen","t":"Lily and Rose are **identical** twins who host a popular cooking show. Viewers often find it hard to **identify** which sister is which, and the twins love to play jokes on them. Despite their looks, however, they have very different personalities, and each has her own **identity** as a cook.\n\nThe show began as a small video channel filmed in their **household** kitchen. Today, it attracts a **huge** audience across Asia. Lily, the more serious twin, specialises in healthy food, while Rose loves sweets and spicy dishes. Their sense of **humor** is a big part of the show's success. Rose likes to explain every recipe using a Chinese **idiom**, such as \"many hands make light work\".\n\nThe twins remain **humble**. They grew up in a poor village where **hunger** was common, and they remember their grandmother teaching them to cook with very little. \"Food is a basic **human** need,\" Lily says, \"but it is also about love.\" They believe that every viewer can create an **ideal** meal at home.\n\nNot every experience has been positive. Once, they filmed an episode during a **humid** summer in which the power failed and the kitchen became a **horrible** mess. Online, some viewers have been **hostile**, leaving rude comments. The twins have learned to **ignore** them. \"Words can **hurt**,\" Rose says, \"but only if you let them.\"\n\nTheir next project is a travel series. They plan to **hunt** for traditional recipes in remote villages. Rose joked that the only real **horror** of travelling is airport food. **However**, both sisters agree that the journey will be worth it. \"As the **hosts|host**, our job is to share joy,\" Lily says."},"44":{"w":["illegal","illness","illustrate","image","imagination","imagine","imitate","immediate","immediately","immense","immigrant","impact","implement","imply","import","importance","impose","impossible","impress","impression"],"title":"The Immigrant Painter","t":"When Ana Silva arrived in China from Brazil twenty years ago, she spoke no Chinese and had very little money. Like many an **immigrant**, she faced an **immense** challenge just to survive. Today, she is one of the most respected painters in Shanghai, and her work has made a deep **impression** on art lovers around the world.\n\nAna's early life in China was hard. She worked as an English teacher and painted at night. She liked to **imitate** traditional Chinese brush painting, then add the bright colours of her home country. Her **imagination** brought the two cultures together. \"I could not **imagine** painting any other way,\" she says.\n\nHer first exhibition had an **immediate** **impact**. Critics said her work could **illustrate** the beauty of cultural exchange, and collectors bought paintings **immediately**. Her **image** of a Chinese garden filled with tropical birds became famous. Some people even said that the painting seemed to **imply** that cultures, like gardens, grow richer with variety.\n\nSuccess brought new problems. Factories began to produce **illegal** copies of her paintings, and it was almost **impossible** to stop them. Ana worked with lawyers to **implement** new copyright protections. Some officials suggested that the government should **impose** heavy fines on those who **import** fake art.\n\nAna's life has not been without difficulty. A serious **illness** forced her to stop working for a year, but she recovered and returned to painting with new energy. She tells young artists about the **importance** of patience. \"To **impress** others, you must first be true to yourself,\" she says."},"45":{"w":["impressive","improve","improvement","include","income","increase","increasingly","independence","independent","indicate","indispensable","individual","indoor","industrial","industry","inevitable","infect","inferior","influence","influential"],"title":"The Rise of Indoor Farming","t":"In recent years, a new **industry** has begun to grow in cities around the world: **indoor** farming. In tall buildings and old factories, vegetables grow under special lights, without soil or sunlight. The **improvement** in technology has made this possible, and the results are **impressive**.\n\nIndoor farms offer several advantages. They use far less water than traditional farms, and they can produce food all year round. They also **include** advanced systems that **indicate** when plants need nutrients. Because crops are grown indoors, they are less likely to be **infected|infect** by pests or diseases, which reduces the need for chemicals.\n\nThe industry is **increasingly** **influential**. Many city governments see indoor farming as **indispensable** for food security. As cities grow, it is almost **inevitable** that farmland will shrink. Indoor farms in old **industrial** buildings can **increase** local food supplies and create jobs. Some critics argue that indoor vegetables taste **inferior** to those grown outdoors, but taste tests suggest the difference is small.\n\nIndoor farming has also changed lives. For many young people, starting a small farm gives them financial **independence**. One **individual** farmer told me that her **income** has doubled since she moved her business indoors. She is now **independent** of weather and seasons. \"I can **improve** my methods every day,\" she said.\n\nExperts believe the industry will have a growing **influence** on how cities feed themselves. It will not replace traditional farming, but it may change the way we think about where food comes from."},"46":{"w":["inform","ingredient","inherit","initial","initiative","injure","injury","ink","innocent","input","insight","insist","inspire","install","instance","instant","institution","instruction","instrument","insufficient"],"title":"The Little Shop of Brushes and Strings","t":"When my grandfather died last year, I was surprised to **inherit** his small shop in the old town. For fifty years, he had sold calligraphy brushes, **ink** and traditional musical instruments. My **initial** reaction was to sell it, but I soon changed my mind.\n\nThe shop was full of history. Every **instrument**, from the two-stringed erhu to the bamboo flute, had a story. Customers came in to **inform** me how my grandfather had helped them choose their first brush or repair a broken string. Their stories gave me a new **insight** into his life. He was not just a shopkeeper; he was a teacher.\n\nRunning the shop was not easy. My knowledge of traditional music was **insufficient**, and I had to learn quickly. I read old **instruction** books and asked older customers for advice. One elderly man, a famous calligrapher, would **insist** on teaching me how to grind ink properly. \"Every **ingredient** matters,\" he said, \"even the water.\"\n\nI also took the **initiative** to modernise. I decided to **install** a small computer system to track stock, and I started selling online. Online orders arrive almost **instantly|instant**, and customers from abroad now buy our brushes. Local schools and a music **institution** have started buying from us too. My friends give valuable **input** on design and marketing.\n\nThere have been setbacks. Once I fell from a ladder and suffered a back **injury**, and I had to rest for weeks. I learned to be careful not to **injure** myself while carrying heavy boxes. For **instance**, I now ask for help with deliveries. Children who visit the shop, with their **innocent** curiosity, always ask to try the instruments. I hope the shop will **inspire** them, as it inspired me."},"47":{"w":["insurance","integrate","intellectual","intelligence","intelligent","intend","intense","intensive","intention","interaction","interest","interfere","internal","international","internet","interpret","interpretation","interpreter","interrupt","interview"],"title":"Will Machines Replace Interpreters?","t":"Last week, I had the chance to **interview** Ms. Zhao, a professional **interpreter** who has worked at **international** conferences for twenty years. With machine translation improving rapidly, I wanted to know her views on the future of her job. Her answers were thoughtful and honest.\n\nMs. Zhao explained that **interpretation** is much more than changing words from one language to another. \"You must **interpret** the speaker's meaning, tone and **intention**,\" she said. \"Sometimes a speaker **intends|intend** to be polite but sounds rude in another language.\" She admitted that artificial **intelligence** is now very good at simple tasks, and that **intelligent** apps on the **internet** can translate menus and signs instantly.\n\nHowever, she believes human skills remain essential. Conference work is **intense** and requires deep **intellectual** effort. Interpreters must listen and speak at the same time, never allowing a pause to **interrupt** the flow. Human **interaction**, she said, depends on understanding culture, humour and emotion, which machines still find difficult.\n\nShe also warned about the risks of relying on machines. If a translation error causes a business deal to fail, who pays? Some companies now buy special **insurance** to protect themselves. She also worries that governments might **interfere** with translation software for political reasons, or that **internal** company documents could be leaked through online tools.\n\nMs. Zhao now teaches an **intensive** course for young interpreters. She encourages them to **integrate** technology into their work rather than fear it. \"Machines can be useful assistants,\" she said. \"But the most important thing is human **interest** in other cultures. That can never be programmed.\""},"48":{"w":["intimate","introduce","introduction","invasion","invent","invention","invest","investigate","investigation","investment","invisible","invitation","invite","involve","irregular","island","isolate","issue","item","jam"],"title":"Smart Homes and Privacy","t":"In recent years, smart home technology has become extremely popular. People can **invest** in devices that turn on lights, lock doors and play music with a simple voice command. The **invention** of these devices has made daily life more convenient. However, they also raise serious concerns about privacy.\n\nMany smart devices **involve** constant listening. To respond to commands, they record sounds in the home. Critics describe this as an **invasion** of privacy, since even **intimate** conversations could be stored on company servers. The technology is almost **invisible**, so users often forget it is there. A recent **investigation** found that some companies kept recordings for years.\n\nGovernments have begun to **investigate** the **issue**. Some have decided to **introduce** new privacy laws, and experts have been asked to give an **introduction** to the risks at public meetings. They warn that hackers could use smart devices to steal information or **isolate** users from their own homes by locking doors remotely. Smart locks can also **jam** after software updates, leaving families stuck outside. Such problems are still **irregular**, but they are growing.\n\nSupporters argue that the benefits are real. Smart homes can help elderly people live independently, and they can save energy. Companies **invite** users to read privacy policies, and some send an **invitation** to join customer panels that test new products. Every new **item** comes with security settings that users can adjust. Engineers continue to **invent** safer systems, and **investment** in security is increasing.\n\nIn my view, technology should serve people, not watch them. Before buying a smart device, consumers should read the rules carefully. Even on a remote **island**, we deserve privacy at home. And when the internet goes down, a simple light switch still works perfectly."},"49":{"w":["jealous","joint","joke","journal","journalist","journey","joy","judge","junior","justice","justify","keen","kid","kill","kindness","knowledge","lab","label","laboratory","lack"],"title":"A Young Journalist's First Story","t":"Last year, my friend Mia began her career as a **junior** **journalist** at a local newspaper. She was **keen** to prove herself, and she hoped to write stories that would make a difference. She soon found her chance.\n\nA reader sent a letter claiming that a popular snack company was using misleading food **labels|label**. The labels said the snacks were \"healthy\", but the reader suspected a serious **lack** of real fruit and far too much sugar. Mia decided to investigate. She bought several packets and took them to a university **laboratory**, where a scientist tested them in her **lab**.\n\nThe results were shocking. The snacks contained very little fruit and large amounts of sugar. Mia wrote a careful article, checked every fact and asked the company to respond. Her editor, an experienced **judge** of news, said the story was strong. \"You must **justify** every claim with evidence,\" he told her. \"That is how we serve **justice** and protect readers.\"\n\nThe story was published in a **joint** investigation with a national **journal**. It caused a sensation. Parents were angry, and some other journalists were **jealous** of Mia's success. The company apologised and promised to change its labels. Mia said the greatest **joy** of her job was knowing she had helped families make better choices for each **kid** at home.\n\nMia keeps a diary of her **journey** as a reporter. She says that **knowledge** is power, but **kindness** matters too; she always treats the people she interviews with respect. When colleagues **joke** that she will soon be famous, she laughs. \"I just want to help **kill** off misleading advertising,\" she says, \"not become a celebrity.\""},"50":{"w":["landlord","lantern","largely","later","latter","launch","law","lawyer","layer","lead","leader","leadership","leading","learning","lecture","legal","legend","legislation","leisure","length"],"title":"Renting in the Big City","t":"For many young people in large cities, renting a home is a major challenge. Rents are high, contracts are complicated, and tenants often feel powerless against their **landlord**. In recent years, however, new **legislation** has begun to improve the situation.\n\nMy friend Lucy learned this the hard way. When she moved to Shanghai, she signed a contract without reading it carefully. **Later**, her landlord tried to raise the rent by thirty percent. Lucy contacted a **lawyer**, who explained that the increase was not **legal** under the new **law**. The **latter** protects tenants from sudden changes, a reform that was **largely** the result of campaigns by student groups.\n\nThe lawyer also gave a free **lecture** to young tenants about their rights. She explained how to read every **layer** of a contract and warned that the **length** of a lease should be clearly stated. \"**Learning** your rights is the best protection,\" she said. Many students in the audience said the talk would **lead** them to check their contracts.\n\nExperts say that good **leadership** from city governments is essential. Some cities have created **leading** programmes that offer affordable housing for young workers. A local **leader** said the aim was to help young people focus on their careers and enjoy their **leisure** time without constant worry about rent.\n\nLucy now lives in a small flat near an old temple. Every year during the **Lantern** Festival, the street is decorated with a red paper **lantern** at every door. Her landlord told her a local **legend** about the lanterns guiding lost travellers home. She also plans to **launch** a website to help other young tenants understand their rights."}});
